  "course_description": "Long historical surveys of national, regional, or ethnic traditions; or of genres or subgenres. Examples: History of African Literature, History of Latinx Literature, History of Science Fiction, History of the Sonnet, History of Southern U.S. Literature, History of Drama.",
  "course_notes": "In this course, we critically analyze Muscogee (Creek) literary written and oral traditions while examining Muscogee history, culture, and contemporary experiences in relation to this literature. We will read and discuss oral stories, non-fiction, fiction, and poetry by Muscogee authors in relation to both pre-removal history in Georgia and Alabama and post-removal history in Indian Territory, the present-day state of Oklahoma. The class is co-taught by Emory Professor Mandy Suhr-Sytsma and College of Muscogee Nation Professor Elizabeth Gray. We will meet in person at Emory and video conference with Professor Gray and CMN students. We will ground our study in principles of relational accountability as we learn in deep, respectful collaboration with our CMN partners.",
